## Solution

**Yes**, $P$ is also a removable singularity for $f$.

**Proof.**

Without loss of generality, translate so that $P = 0$. Each $f_n$ is holomorphic on $D(0,r)\setminus\{0\}$ with a removable singularity at $0$, so each $f_n$ extends to a holomorphic function on all of $D(0,r)$. Equivalently, the Laurent expansion of $f_n$ about $0$ is a Taylor series:

$$f_n(z) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k^{(n)}\, z^k, \qquad |z| < r.$$

The limit $f$ is holomorphic on $D(0,r)\setminus\{0\}$, so it admits a Laurent expansion

$$f(z) = \sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty} a_k\, z^k, \qquad 0 < |z| < r.$$

By Riemann's removable singularity theorem, $0$ is removable for $f$ if and only if $a_k = 0$ for every $k < 0$.

Fix any $\rho$ with $0 < \rho < r$. The circle $\{z : |z| = \rho\}$ is a compact subset of $D(0,r)\setminus\{0\}$, so by hypothesis $f_n \to f$ uniformly on $|z|=\rho$. The Laurent coefficients are recovered by the standard integral formula:

$$a_k = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_{|z|=\rho} \frac{f(z)}{z^{k+1}}\,dz, \qquad a_k^{(n)} = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_{|z|=\rho} \frac{f_n(z)}{z^{k+1}}\,dz.$$

Since $f_n \to f$ uniformly on $|z|=\rho$ and $z^{-(k+1)}$ is continuous (hence bounded) on this circle, we may interchange the limit and the integral:

$$a_k = \lim_{n\to\infty} a_k^{(n)}.$$

Now take any $k < 0$. Since each $f_n$ has a removable singularity at $0$, its Laurent series has no negative-index terms, so $a_k^{(n)} = 0$ for every $n$. Therefore

$$a_k = \lim_{n\to\infty} 0 = 0 \qquad \text{for all } k < 0.$$

The principal part of the Laurent expansion of $f$ vanishes, so $f$ extends holomorphically to $0$. Hence $P$ is a removable singularity for $f$. $\blacksquare$

